**Question 4 (5 points): C Pointers, Arrays & Strings**

Write a C function called `firstChars` that takes just one parameter, an array of strings. The last element in the array of strings will be NULL. The `firstChars` function must return a string containing the first letter of each of the strings in the parameter array. The result of the `firstChars` function must be allocated on the heap. You may not make any assumptions about how many strings will be in the parameter array.

For example, here is a simple main program using the completed `firstChars` function:

```c
int main() {
  char *holidays[] = {"Xmas","Kwanzaa","Hanukkah","NewYears",NULL};
  char *result = firstChars(holidays);
  printf("%s\n", result);
  free(result);
  return 0;
} // end main
```

The output of this program should be
```
XKHN
```

questions.answer:
```c
char *firstChars(char *strings[]) {
    int n = 0;
    while (strings[n] != NULL) {
        n++;
    }
    char *result = malloc(n + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        result[i] = strings[i][0];
    }
    result[n] = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

questions.prompt:
Write a C function called `lastChars` that takes just one parameter, an array of strings. The last element in the array of strings will be NULL. The `lastChars` function must return a string containing the **last** character of each of the strings in the parameter array, in order. Empty strings (`""`) have no last character and contribute nothing. The result must be allocated on the heap. You may not make any assumptions about how many strings will be in the parameter array.

For example:

```c
int main(void) {
  char *fruit[] = {"apple", "", "kiwi", "plum", NULL};
  char *result = lastChars(fruit);
  printf("%s\n", result);
  free(result);
  return 0;
}
```

The output of this program should be
```
eim
```

questions.answer:
```c
char *lastChars(char *strings[]) {
    int n = 0;
    while (strings[n] != NULL) {
        n++;
    }
    char *result = malloc(n + 1);
    int k = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        size_t len = strlen(strings[i]);
        if (len > 0) {
            result[k] = strings[i][len - 1];
            k++;
        }
    }
    result[k] = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

